<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Governorate of Courland</h1><p class="st-meta">1795 CE – 1918 CE · capital Mitau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Russian imperial province formed from the Duchy of Courland in 1795, ruled by its Baltic German knighthood until the First World War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> in 1795, the nobility of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>, and the duchy, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/district-of-pilten">District of Pilten</a>, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Mitau</a> (Jelgava).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> knighthood kept control of local government, courts and the Lutheran church under the Baltic special order, and German remained the language of administration until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> of the 1880s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> was abolished in 1817, but the land stayed largely with the nobles. Latvian peasants increasingly bought farms, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Libau</a> (Liepāja) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ventspils">Windau</a> grew as ports. The 1905 revolution saw many manor houses burned.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>German armies occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> in 1915, and it was administered by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ober-ost">Ober Ost</a>. In 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic Germans</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a> (1918), but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> defeat the territory became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia</a>, with a small southern strip going to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/governorate-of-courland.jpg" alt="Governorate of Courland" width="320" height="311"><figcaption>Image: Пядышев Василий Петрович · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Courland_governorate_1820.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1795 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mitau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latvian, German, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Courland Governorate, Kurland Governorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=governorate-of-courland" aria-label="Governorate of Courland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5193 253 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Governorate of Courland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5513 509L5467 507M5513 509L5513 509M5513 509L6487 457M5513 509L5513 509"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5467 507h0M5513 509h0M6487 457h0M5513 509h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5513 509h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5513 509h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=governorate-of-courland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
